  "hero.title": "How Memory Talks to the CPU",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.sub": "A memory module is labelled DDR5-6400 CL32. The higher the clock, the bigger the latency number. So is it faster or not?<br>When the CPU asks for one byte, what actually happens inside the memory module?",
  "hero.lead": "There is a third question too. Every other wire in the computer has become a fast serial pair (PCIe, SATA, USB). Why does memory still use a wide set of parallel wires? We start with a small capacitor that holds charge: why it leaks, and why a read destroys it. Then how billions of these capacitors are arranged into banks, rows and columns; the five commands the memory controller uses to drive them, and how many cycles each one must wait. Why the CL number keeps growing while the real wait has not got longer. And what limits bandwidth when you move 64 bytes at a time. The second half asks why adding memory modules lowers the clock, what ECC actually protects, how memory soldered to the motherboard differs from memory on a graphics card, and why opening the same row again and again can flip a bit in the next row. <strong>Every model is calculated step by step from real timing parameters</strong>: the timings come from SK hynix and Micron datasheets, refresh and ECC come from the DDR5 standard as quoted in those datasheets, and row hammer comes from papers published since 2014.",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. Every number from a datasheet is labelled with its generation and speed bin. <strong>Where the page says “tuned parameter”, the value was chosen so the model can run. It was not measured</strong>, and the text says how far the conclusion moves if that value doubles. Vendor overclock profiles (XMP) are kept apart from the JEDEC standard bins. This page explains mechanisms only. <strong>It does not recommend any product</strong>, and it does not describe how to carry out a row hammer attack.",
  "ch1.title": "A bit is a leaky capacitor",
  "toc.ch1": "A bit is a leaky capacitor",
  "ch1.law": "A bit in memory is whether a tiny capacitor holds charge. The charge slowly leaks away, and reading it empties it, so the memory must keep reading each row and writing it back. The bigger the chip, the more time this takes.",
  "ch1.p1": "Picture a row of small cups, full for a 1 and empty for a 0. Each cup has a hairline crack in its base, so after a while the water drains out. To check whether a cup is full, you must tip it into a long tube and see whether the water level rises. Once tipped, the cup is empty, and it has to be filled again. This is how DRAM stores data: one transistor works as a switch, and one capacitor acts as the cup. It is called a <strong>1T1C cell</strong>.",
  "ch1.p2": "To read, the switches for a whole row open at once, and each capacitor shares its charge with a long wire that runs to it (the <strong>bit line</strong>). The bit line is much larger than the capacitor, so its voltage moves only a little. A <strong>sense amplifier</strong> at the end of the bit line amplifies that small difference into a full 0 or 1, and writes the value back into the capacitor at the same time. A read is therefore <strong>destructive</strong>, but the amplifier restores every row it reads. Another way to store a bit is a latch: six transistors, no leakage, no refresh, but far more area (see <a href='../switches-that-compute/#ch6'>“How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic”, Chapter 6</a> and <a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch2'>“Why Compute Waits for Memory”, Chapter 2</a>). A flash cell in a solid-state drive keeps its charge behind an insulating layer for years, but it writes slowly and wears out (see <a href='../what-a-drive-is-made-of/#ch4'>“What a Drive Is Made Of”, Chapter 4</a>). A DRAM capacitor loses its charge in tens of milliseconds, in exchange for reads and writes in tens of nanoseconds.",
  "dg.cell.cap": "Figure 1 · One 1T1C cell and its sense amplifier (schematic, not to scale). The word line turns on the transistor, and the capacitor and bit line share charge. The amplifier compares the bit line with the reference line, amplifies the difference and writes it back to the capacitor.",
  "dg.cell.aria": "Structure diagram: a DRAM cell is one transistor and one capacitor. The transistor gate is on the word line and its drain is on the bit line. The bit line goes to a sense amplifier, whose other side connects to a reference bit line. The result is written back after reading.",
  "dg.cell.wl": "Word line (selects a row)",
  "dg.cell.bl": "Bit line",
  "dg.cell.ref": "Reference",
  "dg.cell.t": "Transistor",
  "dg.cell.c": "Capacitor: charged = 1",
  "dg.cell.sa": "Sense amplifier",
  "dg.cell.leak": "Charge leaks",
  "dg.cell.e1": "① The word line rises: every transistor in the row switches on",
  "dg.cell.e2": "② The capacitor shares its charge with the bit line, which moves only a little",
  "dg.cell.e3": "③ The amplifier pushes the difference to full scale, reads 0 or 1, and writes back",
  "ch1.p3": "So the memory controller must send a <strong>refresh</strong> command (REF) regularly. DDR5 rule: send {refN} refreshes within {refMs} ms, which is one every {refi5} µs on average (this interval is called tREFI). DDR4 sends one every {refi4} µs. During a refresh the whole group of chips can do nothing, and that time is called tRFC. The bigger the chip, the more rows it must refresh, so tRFC grows: from {rfc4a} ns for a 2 Gb DDR4 chip to {rfc4} ns at 8 Gb and {rfc4b} ns at 16 Gb.",
  "ch1.model.title": "How much time refresh takes",
  "ch1.model.how": "Pick a chip and a refresh mode to see what share of the chip's time goes to refresh, and how much longer a random read waits on average. tRFC and tREFI are datasheet numbers. The “extra wait” is simple probability: a read lands in a refresh with probability tRFC ÷ tREFI, and once it does, it waits half a tRFC on average.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Bar chart: share of time busy with refresh for several chips. The current choice is highlighted.",
