  "ch1.say.normal": "One refresh every {refi}, each lasting {rfc}: the chip spends <em>{busy}</em> of its time refreshing, and a random read waits {extra} longer on average.",
  "ch1.say.hot": "Above 85°C, charge leaks faster, so the interval halves to {refi}. Busy time doubles to <em>{busy}</em>, and a random read waits {extra} longer on average.",
  "ch1.say.fgr": "Fine-granularity refresh: one every {refi}, each lasting only {rfc}. The total busy share is <em>{busy}</em>, a little more than one full refresh, but each stall is short, so a random read waits only {extra} longer on average.",
  "ch1.say.sb": "One bank at a time: each bank is blocked for {rfc} every {refi}, so that bank is busy <em>{busy}</em> of the time while the others carry on. A random read waits only {extra} longer on average.",
  "ch1.p4": "A default DDR5 16 Gb chip spends {busy5} of its time refreshing, and a random read waits {ext5} ns longer on average. DDR4 at 8 Gb is {busy4}, and at 16 Gb it is {busy4b}. Double the chip and tRFC grows with it, so the refresh share grows too. DDR5 halves the interval to {refi5} µs and offers two remedies. <strong>Fine-granularity refresh</strong> halves the amount refreshed each time ({rfc5f} ns). The total busy share rises slightly to {busy5f}, but the average extra wait falls to {ext5f} ns. <strong>Same-bank refresh</strong> (REFsb) locks only one bank in each group, for {rfc5s} ns. The other banks keep reading and writing, and the average extra wait is only {ext5s} ns. Above 85°C the interval halves again, and the busy share doubles to {busy5h}.",
  "ch1.limit": "The “extra wait” here counts only the case where a read meets a refresh, and it assumes reads arrive at random times. A real memory controller moves refreshes into idle time, or finishes pending reads before refreshing, so the real effect is usually smaller. The tRFC values for 24 Gb and 32 Gb chips are marked “to be determined” in every datasheet I opened, so they are not used here.",
  "ch2.title": "Which row an address lands in",
  "toc.ch2": "Which row an address lands in",
  "ch2.law": "A memory module is not one long line of cells. It has dozens of banks, and each bank has tens of thousands of rows. To read one value, the whole row containing it must first be moved into the row buffer. The memory controller splits the address the CPU gives it into channel, subchannel, bank group, bank, row and column.",
  "ch2.p1": "A library's books are not in one line. First comes the floor, then the shelf, then the row on the shelf, and finally the position within the row. Pull a whole row off the shelf and put it on the table (this is the slow step), and any book on the table is then quick to reach. To fetch from a different row, the current row must go back first.",
  "ch2.p2": "In a DRAM chip, the “shelf” is called a <strong>bank</strong>. A DDR5 16 Gb chip (the x8 kind, which sends 8 bits at a time) has {banks} banks, grouped into {bgs} <strong>bank groups</strong> of {bpg} each. Each bank has {rows} rows, and each row holds {page} bytes (the part of that row inside this chip). Opening a row is called <strong>activation</strong>: all {page} bytes of the row are read by the sense amplifiers and held there. That row of amplifiers is the <strong>row buffer</strong>. Any column in the open row can then be read quickly, until the row is closed and written back.",
  "dg.bank.cap": "Figure 2 · One bank. The row address selects a row, which goes into the row buffer. The column address picks a run from the row buffer and sends it out (schematic, not to scale; row and byte counts from the Micron 16 Gb DDR5 datasheet). RA = row address, CA = column address.",
  "dg.bank.aria": "Structure diagram: a bank is an array of rows and columns. The row decoder selects one row, which is loaded into the row buffer (the sense amplifiers) below. The column select then picks a short run from the row buffer and sends it to the data lines.",
  "dg.bank.rowdec": "Row decoder",
  "dg.bank.array": "{rows} rows × {page} bytes",
  "dg.bank.open": "Activated row",
  "dg.bank.rb": "Row buffer (sense amplifiers)",
  "dg.bank.coldec": "Column select",
  "dg.bank.dq": "Data lines (8 bits per chip)",
  "dg.bank.ra": "RA",
  "dg.bank.ca": "CA",
  "ch2.p3": "A common 16 GB DDR5 memory module is {chips} of these chips side by side, forming a 32-bit <strong>subchannel</strong>. Two subchannels make up one module. The {chips} chips in one subchannel always act together: their same row adds up to {rowKiB} KiB, which holds exactly {linesRow} cache lines of 64 bytes. The CPU usually has two channels, with one module in each. So a physical address must be cut into several fields, and the memory controller decides which bits mean what. A common scheme keeps the lowest few bits for the offset inside a cache line, then moves upward through channel, subchannel, column, bank group and bank. The top dozen or so bits are the row number.",
  "dg.addr.cap": "Figure 3 · One way to cut a {abits}-bit address across two channels, each with one 16 GB single-rank module (“row-first”). BG = bank group, SC = subchannel, Ch = channel. The bit widths come from the chip's row and column counts. The memory controller decides the order of the fields; the model below lets you change it.",
  "dg.addr.aria": "Structure diagram: a 35-bit physical address is cut from high to low into row, bank, bank group, column, subchannel, channel and byte-offset fields, with the bit count of each field marked.",
