  "ch2.say.map": "This address goes to channel {ch}, subchannel {sub}: bank group {bg}, bank {ba} within the group (bank {bank} on the chip), row {row}, and it is block {col} of 64 bytes in that row. The next cache line (address + 64) goes to another channel or subchannel. The next cache line in the same subchannel (address + {step}) lands in {next}.",
  "ch2.say.over": "This address is more than {abits} bits long. Two 16 GB modules cannot hold it, and the extra high bits have nowhere to go.",
  "ch2.say.bad": "Enter a hexadecimal number, for example 0x23A5C9E48.",
  "ch2.next.col": "the same row of the same bank, next column",
  "ch2.next.bg": "the same row in the next bank group",
  "ch2.next.row": "a different bank or a different row",
  "ch2.p4": "The default address 0x23A5C9E48 is cut row-first. It lands in channel {mCh}, subchannel {mSub}, bank group {mBg}, bank {mBa} of the group, row {mRow}, column {mCol}. A {abits}-bit address is exactly {agib} GiB: two {dimmGB} GB modules, each with two subchannels, and {chips} chips of {dens} Gb per subchannel. A real memory controller also XORs some row bits into the bank number, so two large arrays do not land on the same bank by accident. The next chapter shows how bad it is when they do.",
  "ch2.limit": "Only a single-rank module is drawn here (one rank, or side). A double-rank module needs one more bit to choose the side. Each vendor's memory controller uses a scheme that is not published, often with XOR. The two schemes here are only to show what each field controls.",
  "ch3.title": "Five commands, a chain of waits",
  "toc.ch3": "Five commands, a chain of waits",
  "ch3.law": "The memory controller drives memory with only five commands: activate a row (ACT), read (RD), write (WR), close a row (PRE) and refresh (REF). Each command must wait a set number of cycles before the next, and latency is the sum of those waits. If the row you want is already open, the access is fastest. If a different row is open, it is slowest.",
  "ch3.p1": "Back to the library: the book you want is on the row already on the table, so you reach it straight away. If the table is empty, you first fetch a row. If the table holds another row, that row must go back before the new one is fetched. These three cases are called a <strong>row hit</strong>, a <strong>row miss</strong> (the bank is empty) and a <strong>row conflict</strong>.",
  "ch3.p2": "The datasheet gives the wait for each step in clock cycles. Take JEDEC DDR5-6400B as an example (one cycle is {tck6} ps). After sending ACT, wait <strong>tRCD</strong> = {rcd6} cycles before a read. After sending RD, wait <strong>CL</strong> = {cl6} cycles for the data to appear, then use {bl2} cycles to send the 64 bytes. Once a row is open it must stay open for at least <strong>tRAS</strong> = {ras6} cycles before it can close. After sending PRE, wait <strong>tRP</strong> = {rp6} cycles before the next row can be opened. Two ACTs to the same bank must be at least <strong>tRC</strong> = tRAS + tRP = {rc6} cycles apart, which is about {rc6ns} ns.",
  "dg.timing.cap": "Figure 4 · A row conflict on DDR5-6400B, drawn to scale in cycles (numbers from the model's timing table). PRE = precharge (close the row), ACT = activate (open a row), RD = read, CL = CAS latency, CMD = command. A hit has only the last two segments; a miss lacks the first one.",
  "dg.timing.aria": "Timing diagram: the horizontal axis is clock cycles, drawn to scale. It shows PRE, ACT and RD commands. After PRE comes tRP, after ACT comes tRCD, after RD comes CL, then the data burst. The total conflict latency is marked.",
  "dg.timing.cmd": "CMD",
  "dg.timing.data": "Data",
  "dg.timing.pre": "PRE",
  "dg.timing.act": "ACT",
  "dg.timing.rd": "RD",
  "dg.timing.trp": "tRP {n}",
  "dg.timing.trcd": "tRCD {n}",
  "dg.timing.cl": "CL {n}",
  "dg.timing.bl": "Burst {n}",
  "dg.timing.hit": "Hit: {n} cycles = {ns} ns",
  "dg.timing.miss": "Miss: {n} cycles = {ns} ns",
  "dg.timing.conf": "Conflict: {n} cycles = {ns} ns",
  "ch3.p3": "The latency of each case is the sum of its waits. A hit is CL + burst = {hit6c} cycles, {hit6} ns. A miss adds tRCD: {miss6c} cycles, {miss6} ns. A conflict adds tRP as well: {conf6c} cycles, {conf6} ns. The simulator below follows these rules cycle by cycle. It issues at most one command per cycle, and every command must satisfy all the waiting rules (including tRRD, tFAW and tCCD, which later chapters explain).",
  "ch3.model.title": "A cycle-by-cycle DRAM command simulator",
  "ch3.model.how": "Pick an access pattern and a row policy to see how many of the {trN} reads are hits, misses and conflicts, and how long each read waits on average. “Streaming” reads consecutive cache lines (group-first mapping, which rotates through the 8 bank groups in the same row). “Random” picks a bank and row at random each time. “Ping-pong” alternates between two rows that happen to sit in the same bank. The diagram above shows the commands and data of the first few reads. Each read waits for the previous one, like following a pointer. The last cell shows the throughput when all {trN} reads are queued together.",
  "ch3.model.aria": "Timeline: the ACT, RD and PRE commands and data bursts of the first few reads, laid out in time order.",
