  "ch3.cv.cmd": "Command",
  "ch3.cv.t": "Time (ns)",
  "ch3.say.hits": "Mostly row hits ({h} of them): the row needed stays open. The average is <em>{lat}</em> per read, close to the single-read hit time of {hit}.",
  "ch3.say.conflicts": "Mostly conflicts ({c} of them): each read must close the other row before opening its own. The average is <em>{lat}</em> per read.",
  "ch3.say.misses": "{m} misses and {c} conflicts: an open row does not help, because the next access is almost always to another row. The average is <em>{lat}</em> per read.",
  "ch3.say.missclose": "Close at once: the next access always finds the bank empty, so all {m} reads are misses. The average is <em>{lat}</em> per read, and the tRP wait of a conflict is saved.",
  "ch3.p4": "On DDR5-6400B, streaming reads average {sSlat} ns per read. Apart from the first few, which must open rows, they are all hits. Random reads average {sRlat} ns, with {sRmiss} of the {trN} reads missing and {sRconf} conflicting. With close-page, every read is a miss, and the average falls to {sRclat} ns. So neither open nor close page is always better. Open page pays off when access is regular, and close page pays off when it is not. Ping-pong is the worst case. Open page averages {sPlat} ns, and close page still takes {sPclat} ns, because two row openings in the same bank must be at least tRC apart, however they are scheduled. This is the “two large arrays on one bank” problem from the previous chapter.",
  "ch3.p5": "These figures are only the memory chip's own time. From a CPU read to the data arriving, the request also crosses the caches, the on-chip network and the memory controller's queue. A typical desktop processor measures about {dramNs} ns for one memory read (see <a href='../how-cpu-caches-work/#ch1'>“How a CPU's Three Levels of Cache Work”, Chapter 1</a>). The chip's few tens of nanoseconds are a large part of that, but not all of it.",
  "ch3.limit": "The simulator handles reads only. It has no writes (they have their own tWR and read-to-write turnaround waits). It has no refresh (Chapter 1 covers that separately). It issues at most one command per cycle, while DDR5 ACT in practice occupies two cycles; this is simplified to one. The scheduler is the usual “serve hits first, then open rows in arrival order” (FR-FCFS, Rixner et al. 2000). Random accesses are generated from a fixed seed, so every run is the same.",
  "ch4.title": "CL keeps growing, the wait does not",
  "toc.ch4": "CL keeps growing, the wait does not",
  "ch4.law": "CL is a count of cycles, not a time. Double the clock and a cycle is half as long. Double CL, and the wait in nanoseconds stays the same. Over twenty years, real memory latency has barely changed. What has changed is how much data it can move per second.",
  "ch4.p1": "Which is longer, waiting 16 ticks or 32 ticks? It depends on how long a tick is. The DDR4-3200 clock runs at 1600 MHz, so one cycle is {tck4} ns. The DDR5-6400 clock runs at 3200 MHz, so one cycle is {tck5} ns. (“6400” is the number of transfers per second: data moves on both the rising and the falling edge of the clock.) To convert CL to nanoseconds: CL × 2000 ÷ transfer rate.",
  "ch4.p2": "The table lists several speed bins. The JEDEC standard bins come from SK hynix and Micron datasheets. Rows marked “vendor overclock” are profiles set by memory makers, using higher voltages and selected chips (XMP profiles). They are not part of the standard.",
  "ch4.th.bin": "Bin",
  "ch4.th.kind": "Type",
  "ch4.th.t": "CL-tRCD-tRP",
  "ch4.th.cl": "CL in nanoseconds",
  "ch4.th.miss": "Row miss (tRCD + CL)",
  "ch4.th.peak": "Peak per 64-bit channel",
  "ch4.p3": "The vendor-overclocked DDR5-6400 CL32 has a CL of {cl5x} ns. The DDR4-3200 CL16 of ten years ago also gives {cl4x} ns. The JEDEC DDR4-3200AA is {cl43} ns, and the DDR5-6400B is {cl56} ns. So the CL of standard bins also stays between 13 and 17 ns. Over the same period, the peak bandwidth per 64-bit channel rose from {peak4} GB/s to {peak6} GB/s.",
  "ch4.model.title": "CL calculator",
